{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>The nakedness of your father, and the nakedness of your mother.</b> This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 18:7): \"The nakedness of your father\" — this is your father's wife. \"And the nakedness of your mother\" — to include his mother who is not his father's wife. \"The nakedness of your father's wife\" — to include after death. And similarly he explained \"The nakedness of your father's brother you shall not uncover\" (Leviticus 18:14) — and what is his nakedness? \"To his wife you shall not approach.\" And so it is indeed the case according to its correct plain sense.\n\nBut in the Gemara in tractate Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 54a-b) they raised the possibility that \"the nakedness of your father\" and \"the nakedness of your father's brother\" apply to the males themselves literally, and to obligate him for two [sin-offerings] in the case of inadvertent violation. And I am astonished at their view: why would the Torah not give such a warning regarding a son and a brother together with the nakedness of their wives? Perhaps the Torah was more concerned for the honor of elders. But every single verse in the passage constitutes one warning — there do not appear in the passage two warnings in a single prohibition, for the prohibition concerning all the sisters is one, and likewise the daughters are one.\n\nAnd what seems correct in my eyes according to the plain sense is that \"the nakedness of your father and the nakedness of your mother\" is one prohibition and one warning, to prevent him from lying with his mother, for in lying with her he uncovers the nakedness of his father and the nakedness of his mother. And this is what it says (here), \"she is your mother\" — for you have committed two evils with her: you uncovered the nakedness of a mother and the nakedness of a father. And after it warned about the nakedness of his father who is his mother, it went back and warned about the nakedness of his father's wife who is not his mother, and said (Leviticus 18:8), \"The nakedness of your father's wife you shall not uncover; it is the nakedness of your father.\" And likewise, \"The nakedness of your father's brother you shall not uncover\" — its meaning is \"to his wife you shall not approach,\" and therefore it did not say \"and to.\""], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The reference to Sanhedrin is to 54a-b, where the Gemara derives that the prohibitions of 'the nakedness of your father' and 'the nakedness of your father's brother' apply to the male relatives themselves."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:18.211535895Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}